{"explanation":"Rule: the grappling entry says that when you want to grab a creature, you can use the Attack action to make a special melee attack, a grapple, replacing one of the attacks that action grants. The opportunity attack entry says you use your reaction, not the Attack action, to make one melee attack against the provoking creature. Fact: you want to grapple as your opportunity attack, which happens on your reaction rather than as part of the Attack action. Conclusion: on the natural reading, no. The grapple is made available through the Attack action, and an opportunity attack is not that action.

The conclusion turns on the phrase 'one melee attack' in the opportunity attack entry, read against 'you can use the Attack action to make a special melee attack, a grapple.' The text allows another reading. A grapple is expressly called a 'special melee attack', so it could fall within 'one melee attack'. On that reading, the Attack action is only the usual way to grapple, not the only way, and you could grapple on your reaction.

Under the first reading, the grapple exists only as a replacement for an attack within the Attack action, so the answer is no. Under the second, any permission to make a melee attack covers a grapple, so the answer is yes. The entries never say whether 'melee attack' elsewhere includes the special attacks, or whether the Attack action is required, so the text does not settle which reading is correct.","ambiguous":true,"answer":{"kind":"yes-or-no","verdict":"no"},"cites":["making-an-attack > melee-attacks > opportunity-attacks","making-an-attack > melee-attacks > grappling"]}
